WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 231: The Netherlands: The Dutch Golden Age: The VOC, Tulip Mania, and Amsterdam's Canals

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.000
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:18.560
[Rebecca] Today, we step into the bustling wharves, opulent townhouses, and smoky art studios of 17th century Amsterdam to explore the astonishing zenith of human commerce, art and innovation.

3
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.040
[Rebecca] The Dutch Golden Age.

4
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:34.560
[George] In the 17th century, a tiny republic of barely two million people, having thrown off the Spanish imperial yoke, performed an economic miracle that stunned the civilized world.

5
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.360
[George] Amsterdam became the financial, maritime, and trading heart of the planet.

6
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:50.800
[George] Through ingenious land reclamation techniques utilizing hundreds of windmills, the Dutch drained lakes and pushed back the sea, carving out fertile agricultural polders beneath sea level.

7
00:00:50.800 --> 00:01:03.600
[George] To house their booming merchant population, the city fathers engineered the Grachtinggordel, a magnificent concentric crescent of three grand canals, the Heerngracht, Kiesersgracht, and Princingracht.

8
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:18.640
[George] Thousands of slender, four-story brick townhouses with ornate stepped gables arose along the waterways, equipped with projecting roof beams and hoisting hooks to haul spices, grain and textiles directly from cargo barges into attic warehouses.

9
00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:35.520
[George] The Amsterdam Stock Exchange, founded in 1602, became the first permanent stock market in world history, while the Bank of Amsterdam pioneered modern central banking and secure credit transfers, turning Amsterdam into the undisputed commercial capital of the globe.

10
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:51.360
[George] The wooden foundation piles beneath Amsterdam's grand canal houses were driven deep into the soft peat mud by heavy mechanical drop hammers powered by teams of horses, requiring over 13,000 Norwegian spruce piles for the royal palace on the dam square alone.

11
00:01:51.360 --> 00:02:04.280
[George] The Hearngracht was nicknamed the Gentleman's Canal, because it attracted the wealthiest regents and VOC directors, who competed to construct the most opulent sandstone facades and private Baroque garden pavilions.

12
00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:14.200
[George] The engine of Dutch maritime supremacy was the very nigg Oostendische Compagny, the Dutch East India Company, or VOC, founded in 1602.

13
00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:23.720
[George] The VOC was a revolutionary economic invention, the world's first publicly traded multinational corporation with limited shareholder liability.

14
00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:36.920
[George] Empowered by the Dutch States General with sovereign powers to wage war, build fortresses, mint coins, and negotiate treaties, the VOC deployed vast fleets of armed merchantmen known as East Indiamen.

15
00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:44.680
[George] They broke the Portuguese trade monopoly in the Indian Ocean, seizing Malacca, Ceylon, and the spice-rich Malacas.

16
00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:57.800
[George] In modern-day Indonesia, Governor General Jan Pieters Zun Cohen founded the fortified colonial capital of Batavia, securing total global monopolies over nutmeg, cloves, mace and cinnamon.

17
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:18.360
[George] At its peak, the VOC commanded a fleet of hundreds of merchant ships, tens of thousands of employees, and paid annual dividends of 40% to its Dutch investors, accumulating an aggregate value that, in modern currency, surpassed $7 trillion, making it the most valuable commercial enterprise in human history.

18
00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:32.840
[George] VOC ships sailing the treacherous four month voyage around Africa were provisioned with sauerkraut, beer, and salted beef to ward off scurvy, while shipboard surgeons maintained meticulous medical journals detailing tropical fevers.

19
00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:45.360
[George] VOC ships were armed with heavy cast iron cannons to defend their precious cargoes of nutmeg and cloves from pirate attacks and rival European privateers across the pirate-infested waters of the Malacca Strait.

20
00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:52.160
[George] The technological cornerstone of Dutch dominance was a humble cargo vessel known as the Flight, or Flute Ship.

21
00:03:52.400 --> 00:04:03.920
[George] Unlike traditional Spanish or Portuguese galleons, which were heavily armed floating fortresses requiring massive crews, the Dutch designed the flight purely for commercial cargo efficiency.

22
00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:20.560
[George] With a distinctive pear-shaped hull, flat bottom, and narrow upper deck, engineered specifically to minimize Danish tolls in the Baltic Sound, which were taxed based on upper deck width, the flight carried twice the cargo of competing European vessels with half the crew.

23
00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:33.600
[George] Utilising sophisticated block and tackle rigging and shipboard winches, a crew of just twelve Dutch sailors could handle a 300-ton flight, whereas a comparable English or French ship required 30 men.

24
00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:48.880
[George] This drastic reduction in labor and operating costs allowed Dutch merchants to offer shipping rates that no competitor in Europe could match, turning the Dutch merchant fleet into the wagoneers of all Europe, carrying more than half of all international oceanic cargo.

25
00:04:48.880 --> 00:05:02.800
[George] The Dutch shipbuilding industry along the Zahn River was the first to implement prefabricated assembly lines powered by industrial wind sawmills, producing a finished merchant ship every single week to satisfy global trading demand.

26
00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:14.720
[George] The timber yards along the Zahn River employed thousands of skilled craftsmen who used specialized steamboxes to bend thick oak planks for the bulbous, pear shaped hulls of Dutch flight merchant vessels.

27
00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:23.760
[George] As unprecedented wealth poured into the Dutch Republic, it fueled the most famous speculative frenzy in economic history, tulip mania.

28
00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:38.760
[George] Introduced from the Ottoman Empire in the late 16th century, the tulip became the ultimate luxury status symbol among wealthy Dutch burgers, prized especially for rare broken colour patterns caused by a harmless plant mosaic virus.

29
00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:49.080
[George] By the 1630s, the trade in tulip bulbs transitioned into professional futures contracts traded in smoky backrooms of taverns across Harlem and Amsterdam.

30
00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.880
[George] Prices skyrocketed to insane heights.

31
00:05:51.880 --> 00:06:02.520
[George] A single viceroy bulb was sold for four fat oxen, eight pigs, twelve sheep, two tons of butter, one thousand pounds of cheese, and a silver drinking goblet.

32
00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:16.440
[George] The ultimate prize, the rare Semper Augustus, a striking red flame on white petals, commanded 5,500 guilders, enough money to purchase a grand four-story townhouse on Amsterdam's finest canal.

33
00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:23.080
[George] Chimney sweeps, tailors and sailors mortgaged their homes and tools to speculate on paper bulb contracts.

34
00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:30.440
[George] But in February 1637, at an auction in Harlem, a lot of bulbs failed to fetch the reserve price.

35
00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:32.360
[George] Panic struck the market.

36
00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:44.680
[George] Overnight, buyers vanished, contracts were repudiated, and bulb prices crashed by 95%, leaving thousands of speculators ruined in history's first modern financial crash.

37
00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:59.240
[George] Notaries across Holland were overwhelmed with lawsuits when the tulip market collapsed, leading provincial courts to declare all futures contracts unenforceable gambling debts, which allowed merchants to settle their ruinous debts for pennies on the guilder.

38
00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:13.400
[George] The tulip boom was catalyzed by the devastating bubonic plague of 1636, which created an atmosphere of fatalistic abandon that encouraged ordinary working burgers to gamble their life savings on speculative flower futures.

39
00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:19.040
[George] The immense wealth of the Dutch Republic created an unprecedented domestic market for fine art.

40
00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:32.000
[George] For the first time in European history, art was purchased not by kings, popes or feudal dukes, but by ordinary merchants, bakers, butchers, and guilds who hung oil paintings in their parlors.

41
00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:44.000
[George] In the center of this cultural explosion stood Rembrandt van Reyen, the undisputed master of Carascuro, the dramatic interplay of light and shadow, in works like The Anatomy Lesson of Dr.

42
00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:57.840
[George] Nicolese Tulpe and the Monumental Night Watch, commissioned by the Amsterdam Civic Militia, Rembrandt revolutionized portraiture by injecting psychological vitality, movement, and human emotion into corporate scenes.

43
00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:08.000
[George] Rather than painting static rows of stiff aristocrats, Rembrandt captured living human beings breathing, talking, and stepping out of the canvas into divine light.

44
00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:23.680
[George] Alongside Rembrandt, Johannes Vermeer in Delft painted sublime, quiet domestic scenes bathed in delicate morning window light, such as Girl with a Pearl Earring, capturing the serene, bourgeois dignity of Dutch 17th century life.

45
00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:36.960
[George] Rembrandt's fascination with historical authenticity led him to collect turbans, Roman breastplates, and exotic Eastern weaponry from Amsterdam's Jewish quarter to dress his models with unparalleled dramatic realism.

46
00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:50.320
[George] In the Night Watch, Rembrandt broke traditional guild conventions by depicting Captain Franz Banning Kock and his lieutenant Willem van Reitenberch in mid stride, their yellow silk uniforms glowing against the shadows.

47
00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:56.720
[George] The Dutch Republic was an oasis of intellectual freedom in a continent suffocated by religious censorship.

48
00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:03.720
[George] Thinkers, scientists, and political refugees from across Europe found safe haven in Amsterdam and Leiden.

49
00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:13.800
[George] The Jewish philosopher Baruk Spinoza ground optical lenses while developing revolutionary pantheistic philosophy and championing modern democratic tolerance.

50
00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:18.040
[George] In jurisprudence, Hugo Grotius published De Jure Belli A.

51
00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:18.280
[George] C.

52
00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:29.640
[George] Passus, on the law of war and peace, and Mer Liberum, the Free Sea, establishing the foundational principles of modern international law and freedom of navigation on the high seas.

53
00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:41.640
[George] In science, Christian Hygienes invented the pendulum clock, patented the spiral balance spring for pocket watches, discovered Saturn's moon Titan, and formulated the wave theory of light.

54
00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:57.480
[George] In Delft, Antony van Leeuenhoek ground tiny, perfect glass microscope lenses, discovering single-celled protozoa and bacteria in pond water, which he called animalcules, unlocking the microscopic universe for human understanding.

55
00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:09.720
[George] Christian hygienes ground his own compound telescope lenses in his workshop in The Hague, observing the rings of Saturn and inventing the micrometric screw to measure the precise diameters of distant planets.

56
00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:25.400
[George] In Delft, Antony van Leeuenhoek ground over five hundred tiny glass lenses, discovering microscopic red blood cells, spermatozoa, and the intricate segmented eyes of flies, corresponding regularly with the Royal Society in London.

57
00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:35.240
[George] The golden zenith of the Republic met an existential crisis in 1672, remembered in Dutch history as the ramp jar, the disaster year.

58
00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:47.120
[George] The Sun King Louis XIV of France invaded the Netherlands with a colossal army of 120,000 soldiers, while England attacked from the sea and German Prince Bishops invaded from the east.

59
00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:54.000
[George] In the panic, the Dutch Prime Minister Johan de Witt was brutally murdered by an enraged mob in The Hague.

60
00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:59.360
[George] In desperation, the young Prince William III of Orange was appointed stadtholder.

61
00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:03.440
[George] William ordered the catastrophic activation of the Dutch waterline.

62
00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:13.840
[George] Slouse gates were opened and dikes demolished, turning vast swathes of Holland into an impassable, shallow inland lake that French cavalry and artillery could not cross.

63
00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:17.680
[George] The French invasion was halted, the Republic was saved.

64
00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:18.880
[George] William I.I.

65
00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:28.640
[George] later married Mary Stuart and ascended the English throne in the glorious revolution of 1688, uniting Dutch and English power against French absolutism.

66
00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:39.200
[George] The Dutch Golden Age left an eternal legacy of democratic constitutionalism, maritime audacity, and artistic brilliance that helped create the modern Western world.

67
00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:51.520
[George] The flooded plains of the Dutch waterline created an impassable watery barrier five miles wide and just deep enough to drown marching infantry while remaining too shallow for heavy French warships to navigate.

68
00:11:51.520 --> 00:12:04.800
[George] Prince William III's strategic flooding of the Dutch waterline demonstrated the unyielding resolve of the Dutch people, proving that they would rather drown their own fertile homeland than surrender to foreign royal absolutism.

69
00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:16.480
[Rebecca] Next week, our European journey sails south to the Atlantic shores of the Iberian Peninsula with episode 232.

70
00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:22.640
[Rebecca] Portugal Part 1, Prince Henry the Navigator, Vasco de Gama, and the Sea Route to India.

71
00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:32.440
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

